%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C9037705] # Descriptive Statistics
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] For a review of how to work with live scripts, check out [Starting Tips](file:../../StartingTips.m).
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:3c08)
%[text]{"align":"center"} A gentoo penguin using its flippers to [dive](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gentoo_Penguin._swimming_(10909435215).jpg). 
%[text] Make sure you've gone through the previous live scripts in this module. If `penguins1` is no longer in your workspace, this block of code will get everything set up for this activity: 
filename = "penguins.csv";
penguins = readtable(filename);
penguins.species = categorical(penguins.species);
penguins.island = categorical(penguins.island);
penguins.sex = categorical(penguins.sex);
penguins1 = rmmissing(penguins);
chinstrap = penguins1(penguins1.species == "Chinstrap",:);
gentoo = penguins1(penguins1.species == "Gentoo",:); 
adelie = penguins1(penguins1.species == "Adelie",:);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C8C153F0] ## Overview of descriptive statistics
%[text] Don't worry, you already know a lot of [descriptive statistics](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/descriptive-statistics.html)! 
%[text] For example, a few statistics we might use to describe our data are: 
%[text] - the [minimum](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.min.html) or [maximum](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.max.html) value of your data set (as a bonus, if you subtract the min from the max you'll get the [range](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/range.html) of your dataset)
%[text] - the [mean](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.mean.html) value (average of your data)
%[text] - the [median](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.median.html) value (midpoint of your data if you order all data points in numerical order)
%[text] - the [mode](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.mode.html) value (the value that appears most often in your data set)
%[text] - the [standard deviation](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.std.html) of your data (a measure of the spread of your distribution, or how far the majority of values tend to lie from the mean) \
%[text] Lucky for us, there's a built-in MATLAB® function for each of these methods! 
%[text] First, let's pull back up the histogram so we can easily see how these statistics map onto it.
histogram(penguins1.flipper_length_mm)
xlabel("Flipper Length (mm)")
ylabel("Frequency")
title("Distribution of penguin flipper lengths")
%[text] Find the minimum flipper length in the population using `min()`:
min_flip = min(penguins1.flipper_length_mm)  %[output:57c7f6d0]
xline(min_flip, "LineWidth",5) %[output:63c9d07a]
%[text] Find the maximum flipper length in the population using `max()`:
max_flip = max(penguins1.flipper_length_mm) %[output:6ab8c824]
xline(max_flip, "LineWidth",5) %[output:63c9d07a]
%[text] The minimum and maximum values have been plotted as vertical lines on our histogram. The built-in function [`xline()`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/xline.html) creates a vertical line at a given constant value of x. 
%[text] Looking at this data, we can say that all of our flipper length values lie between 172 and 231 mm (this is the range of our dataset). 
%%
%[text] Next, let's find the mean, median, and mode of our dataset: 
histogram(penguins1.flipper_length_mm, "EdgeColor","none") % you can remove the edges by utilzing the "EdgeColor" option %[output:9040f892]
xlabel("Flipper Length (mm)") %[output:9040f892]
ylabel("Frequency") %[output:9040f892]
title("Distribution of penguin flipper lengths") %[output:9040f892]
mean_flip = mean(penguins1.flipper_length_mm) %[output:24be0c6c]
xline(mean_flip, "--") %[output:9040f892]

median_flip = median(penguins1.flipper_length_mm) %[output:48814e14]
xline(median_flip, ":", "LineWidth",1.5) %[output:9040f892]
mode_flip = mode(penguins1.flipper_length_mm) %[output:2281825e]
xline(mode_flip, "-.", 'LineWidth',2) %[output:9040f892]
legend ("flip length", "mean", "median", "mode") %[output:9040f892]
%[text] We plotted the mean, median, and mode with slightly different line styles so that we can visualize them on our histogram. Note that the mode is at 190 mm, which you'll notice is the highest ('most frequent') value on the histogram. 
%[text] Finally, we can compute the standard deviation using `std()`: 
std_flip = std(penguins1.flipper_length_mm) %[output:3fbcad3a]
%[text] We can therefore say that the majority of flipper length values within our population tend to lie within 14 mm from the mean, which is 201 mm. Most penguins have a flipper length between 187 mm and 215 mm. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9627B295] ## ![](text:image:4e9c) Exercise: Use descriptive statistics to summarize data from one species
%[text] Pick your favorite penguin species and characteristic and calculate these descriptive statistics. What do they tell you about your data? 
%[text] **Hint:** if you pick Chinstrap penguins and want to describe the population's body weight, to plot the histogram and then find the minimum body mass you would run: 
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] histogram(chinstrap.body_mass_g)
%[text] min_chin_mass = min(chinstrap.body_mass_g)
%[text] ```
%[text] Your code here: 
PenguinType = "adelie"; %[control:dropdown:63bf]{"position":[15,23]}
switch PenguinType
    case "adelie"
        PenguinName = "Adelie";
        PenguinTable = adelie;
    case "gentoo"
        PenguinName = "Gentoo";
        PenginTable = gentoo;
    case "chinstrap"
        PenguinName = "Chinstrap";
        PenguinTable = chinstrap;
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A6514EBD] **A:  Plot the histogram for comparison (make sure to add labels and a title).** 
% Example code if you chose Adelie penguins and body mass.

histogram(PenguinTable.body_mass_g) % A %[output:73ac2b8e]
xlabel("Body mass (g)") %[output:73ac2b8e]
ylabel("Frequency") %[output:73ac2b8e]
title("Distribution of body mass in " + PenguinName + " population") %[output:73ac2b8e]
disp("The body masses of " + PenguinName + " have the following values in grams:") %[output:18055e4d]
%%
%[text] **B: Find the minimum value.** 
min_mass = min(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:4e8a587d]
%%
%[text] **C: Find the maximum value.** 
max_mass = max(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:5fb1b45b]
%%
%[text] **D: Find the average of the dataset (mean).** 
mean_mass = mean(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:3ba79c3d]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B36AE2ED] **E: Find the midpoint of the ordered dataset (median).** 
median_ass = median(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:09139617]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0C783F15] **F: Find the most frequent value in the dataset (mode).** 
mode_mass = mode(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:67f7a2af]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3049ACED] **G: Find the standard deviation of your dataset.** 
std_mass = std(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:5820f161]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3011AAA4] **BONUS: What's the range of this dataset?**
rng_mass = range(PenguinTable.body_mass_g)  %[output:0502b2fd]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E9371386] ##  Further resources: 
%[text] 1. For a more mathematical approach to descriptive statistics, check out the courseware [Descriptive Statistics and Probability Distributions](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/95463-descriptive-statistics-and-probability-distributions).
%[text] 2. The [Statistics Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/statistics-onramp/orst) offers more practice with descriptive statistics in MATLAB.
%[text] 3. Learn how to calculate multiple descriptive statistics at once using the MATLAB function [`grpstats()`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/grpstats.html).  \
